	abstract = {A potential model is applied for the analysis of the astrophysical direct nuclear capture process \${\textasciicircum}\{16\}\$O(p,\${\textbackslash}gamma){\textasciicircum}\{17\}\$F. The phase-equivalent potentials of the Woods-Saxon form for the p\$-{\textasciicircum}\{16\}\$O interaction are examined which reproduce the binding energies and the empirical values of {ANC} for the \${\textasciicircum}\{17\}\$F(5/2\${\textasciicircum}+\$) ground and \${\textasciicircum}\{17\}\$F(1/2\${\textasciicircum}+\$) (\$E{\textasciicircum}*\$=0.495 {MeV}) excited bound states from different sources. The best description of the experimental data for the astrophysical \$S\$ factor is obtained within the potential model which yields the {ANC} values of 1.043 fm\${\textasciicircum}\{-1/2\}\$ and 75.484 fm\${\textasciicircum}\{-1/2\}\$ for the \${\textasciicircum}\{17\}\$F(\$5/2{\textasciicircum}\{+\}\$) ground and \${\textasciicircum}\{17\}\$F(\$1/2{\textasciicircum}\{+\}\$) excited bound states, respectively. The zero-energy astrophysical factor \$S(0)=9.321\$ {KeV} b is obtained by using the asymptotic expansion method of D. Baye. The calculated reaction rates within the region up to 10\${\textasciicircum}\{10\}\$ K are in good agreement with those from the R-matrix approach and the Bayesian model in both absolute values and temperature dependence.},
